{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe 2.08 places them in three separate superfamilies under the fold f.21, “Heme-binding four-helical bundle” (SUNID 81344): transmembrane di-heme cytochromes, f.21.1 (81342); fumarate-reductase respiratory-complex transmembrane subunits, f.21.2 (81343); and respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain, f.21.3 (103501). Their shared class is f, “Membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides” (56835), despite their helical architecture.

Thus SCOPe supports a shared fold, not one homologous superfamily. Families group more closely related proteins; superfamilies indicate probable common ancestry, potentially despite weak sequence similarity. Fold and class membership alone do not establish homology. Separate superfamilies mean that common ancestry across these groups is not established by this classification, rather than proving that distant ancestry is impossible.

